1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,720
Rainy day fatigue isn't laziness, it's your hormones and blood vessels reacting to the weather.

2
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:12,960
When cloudy, low-pressure days stack up, plenty of people feel drained and can't figure out why, but there are real physical reasons behind it.

3
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:20,080
First, less sunlight means your body produces less serotonin, the hormone that keeps you feeling alert and motivated.

4
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:32,080
Second, when barometric pressure drops before a storm, blood vessels in your brain dilate and release a burst of serotonin, and it's that swing, not the rain itself, that studies link to headaches and brain fog.

5
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:38,560
One analysis found nearly three in four migraine sufferers name falling pressure as their top weather trigger.

6
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:47,040
Third, low pressure also nudges your body toward rest mode, dropping your blood pressure and leaving you sluggish even after a full night's sleep.

7
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:56,480
To fight back, get outside or sit near a bright window first thing in the morning, even under clouds, since light exposure is still the strongest serotonin trigger you have.

8
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:06,560
Keep a consistent sleep and wake time so your body clock doesn't drift, and stay ahead with light exercise, which helps stabilize your mood on the gloomiest days.
